 {:name "conditionally-convergent-series-use-ordered-partial-sums"
  :level :lemma-location
  :status :draft
  :body
  {:problem-class
   "Formalize the value of a conditionally convergent real series such as the alternating harmonic series."
   :gotcha
   "Mathlib's default `HasSum f a` uses the `unconditional` summation filter: convergence of sums over arbitrary sufficiently large finite subsets, invariant under permutation. It is not the usual ordered-series convergence along initial segments of ℕ."
   :explicit-library-warning
   "`Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.Defs` explicitly names `fun n : ℕ => (-1 : ℝ)^n / (n + 1)` as a function that does not have a sum under the default definition, although it is summable for the conditional filter."
   :wrong-formulation
   "`HasSum (fun n : ℕ => (-1 : ℝ)^n / (n + 1)) (Real.log 2)` with the default filter is false, not merely difficult to prove."
   :correct-formulation
   "`Tendsto (fun n => ∑ i ∈ Finset.range n, (-1 : ℝ)^i / (i + 1)) atTop (𝓝 (Real.log 2))` records convergence in the natural order. An explicit conditional summation filter is another possible formulation when its API is desired."
   :process-consequence
   "Before using `HasSum`, `Summable`, or `tsum` for a non-absolutely-convergent series, inspect the summation filter. If the mathematics depends on term order, state the theorem with ordered partial sums or an explicit conditional filter."
