 {:name "reduce-lattice-periodic-entire-functions-to-compact-domain"
  :level :strategy
  :status :draft
  :maturity :one-shot-heuristic
  :body
  {:problem-class
   "Show that an entire complex-valued function invariant under a rank-two lattice is constant."
   :proof-shape
   ["Extend each base-period equation from one translate to every integer multiple, including negative integers."
    "Choose a compact fundamental parallelogram for the lattice."
    "Decompose every z into a representative w in that parallelogram plus an integer lattice translate. For periods 1 and I, use `Int.fract z.re`, `Int.fract z.im`, `Int.floor z.re`, and `Int.floor z.im`."
    "Use periodicity to prove `f z = f w`, hence `Set.range f` is contained in the image of the compact fundamental domain."
    "Continuity of the coordinate map and f makes that image compact and therefore bounded."
    "Apply Liouville's bounded-entire-function theorem to conclude that f is constant."]
   :rectangular-lattice-decomposition
   "`z = (fract (re z) + fract (im z) * I) + floor (re z) + floor (im z) * I`, with both fractional coordinates in `[0,1]`."
   :formal-assembly
   {:integer-periods
    "`Int.induction_on` extends `f (z+ω)=f z` to `f (z+n*ω)=f z`; `push_cast` and `ring` normalize successor and predecessor arithmetic."
    :compactness
    "`isCompact_Icc.prod isCompact_Icc`, followed by two `IsCompact.image` steps for the coordinate map and f."
    :range-bound
    "`IsBounded.subset` transports boundedness from the compact image to `Set.range f`."
    :final-leaf
    "`Differentiable.exists_const_forall_eq_of_bounded`."}
   :scope
   "The committed instance uses the rectangular lattice generated by 1 and I. A general pair of real-linearly independent periods needs the corresponding coordinate decomposition, but the compact-domain and Liouville architecture is unchanged."
   :failure-boundary
   "Compactness alone bounds f only on the chosen domain; the range-containment proof through lattice reduction is the essential bridge."}
